{"case_id":"T8076","gap_exists":true,"gap_type":["entry_point","API_protocol","state","input_semantics","attack_surface"],"confidence":99,"affected_functions":["ares_dns_parse","ares_dns_parse_rr_opt","ares_dns_rr_set_opt","ares_dns_rr_set_opt_own","ares_dns_rr_get_opt_cnt","ares_dns_rr_get_opt","ares_dns_rr_get_opt_byid","ares_dns_write_rr_opt","ares__dns_options_free"],"reason":"H0 invokes only record-specific legacy reply parsers and has no OPT-specific parser call. S1 adds variable-length EDNS OPT option parsing to the complete-message path documented by ares_dns_parse, plus option collection mutation, lookup, serialization, and destruction. Consequently H0 does not exercise malformed or repeated option tuples, collection growth/replacement, accessor boundary behavior, write-back, or cleanup of the new option-owned allocations.","evidence":["H0 calls ares_parse_a_reply, ares_parse_aaaa_reply, ares_parse_ptr_reply, ares_parse_ns_reply, ares_parse_srv_reply, ares_parse_mx_reply, ares_parse_txt_reply, ares_parse_soa_reply, ares_parse_naptr_reply, ares_parse_caa_reply, and ares_parse_uri_reply; it never calls the declared complete-message entry point ares_dns_parse and contains no OPT operation.","ares_dns_parse_rr_opt now loops over RDATA as repeated big-endian 16-bit option IDs and lengths, duplicates len bytes with a terminator, and inserts them through ares_dns_rr_set_opt_own.","ares_dns_rr_set_opt_own introduces stateful behavior for duplicate-ID replacement, power-of-two reallocations, ownership transfer, and option counts.","ares_dns_rr_get_opt and ares_dns_rr_get_opt_byid add index and identifier lookup semantics, including optional output pointers and sentinel failure behavior, which H0 never invokes.","ares_dns_write_rr_opt now serializes every ARES_RR_OPT_OPTIONS entry, while ares__dns_options_free recursively frees their values; H0 neither constructs/writes an OPT record nor explicitly traverses these APIs."],"recommended_action":"Add a complete-DNS-message fuzz target around ares_dns_parse that generates OPT RDATA with truncated headers, inconsistent lengths, empty values, duplicate option IDs, and many options, then traverses ARES_RR_OPT_OPTIONS using both getters, serializes the record through the DNS writer, reparses it, and destroys all records. Also add an API-sequence mode exercising ares_dns_rr_set_opt replacement, zero-length/null values, collection growth, invalid keys, and out-of-range indexes."}
